format session-log-v1
stamp 20260820.030805
editor Claude Code
model claude-opus-5
voice Kyri
host Vultr SEA NixOS VPS ai-jail pier (Daylight DC-1 hand) -- Eastern (EDT)
title REDS %96 and %97 -- the head stamp nobody checked, and the ledger scan that read 96 rows as zero
prompt Pick up the running thread, close open agent-closable reds first, then take the next Lindy-first crux-first lap.
think Read KYRI and RADIANT_STYLE to stand in the voice, then the operator card, the three newest logs, and the reds ledger. The reds queue read clear -- %90 closed on the host rebuild, %91 superseded by %93 and %94, %95 closed the lap before -- so the thread pointed at the next Caravan rung: what a supervisor does with a plan its own run reports short.
think Read the council rota doc for this lap, index 12 of 27 by log count -- active-designing/20260618-184912_growing-a-language.md. Its centre line is the one the Caravan ladder already lives by: a complex system that works is invariably found to have grown from a simpler system that worked. Directly on point for the next rung, and it stayed in view for the whole lap.
think Reading the clock to stamp the lap returned 20260820.025215 -- EARLIER than the newest log in the index, which reads 20260820.031500. A stamp read from a real clock can never exceed the moment it was written, so one of the two numbers was invented. Standfast: the constructive lap stops here.
think Measured rather than argued. Compared each recent log's stamp against the commit that carried it: 20260820.021400 landed two minutes BEHIND its commit, honestly; 20260820.024612 stood ten minutes ahead of a commit made at 023622; 20260820.031500 stood twenty-eight minutes ahead of a commit made at 024638. Zero, then ten, then twenty-eight. A drift that grows lap over lap is the signature of a stamp estimated from the lap before it rather than read -- the class REDS %63 named and the live-clock-stamps card exists to prevent. Older rows show a four-hour offset instead, which is the pre-%90 UTC window and a different thing entirely.
think Ran tools/one_clock_witness.rish expecting it to be RED. It ran GREEN, printing TRUE_HEAD 20260820.031500 beside a host clock of 20260820.025340 in the same output -- twenty-two minutes apart, on one screen, and the witness called it proven. That is the real finding: the fault was already visible and no duty was asking the question.
think Walked all four duties for the root. Shape reads filenames. Monotonicity can never catch a forward jump, since every false future is monotonic with itself -- the scan says so in its own header. The zone duty weighs the host rather than any stamp. And duty 4, built for exactly this, takes its base from origin/main -- so it weighs a dated artifact for precisely as long as that artifact stays unmerged, and every lap that lands and merges retires its own stamps from the diff forever. The one number the whole law rests on, the living head, was the number nobody checked.
think Built duty 5 rather than tightening an existing one, because the missing question is its own question. one_clock_head_scan.sh asks whether the newest living stamp stands at or behind the live clock within a tight 120s grace -- honest, because a lap stamps, works, then runs the witness, so an honest head is already minutes in the past by the time this runs. It reads the head from the monotonicity scan rather than growing a second walk, so there is one definition of head and no room for the two to drift apart.
think Proved three paths on metal, not one. A stamp read from the live clock passes at a 0s delta. A stamp four hours ahead is refused by name. And a false future planted as a real living log in session-logs/ makes the duty name that exact stamp and refuse -- a fixture proves the arithmetic, a planted control proves the walk.
think The two stamps already written are pinned in one_clock_head_erratum.txt with their cause. Accrete-never-break decides this: dated logs keep their names, the erratum records the fault, and the guard catches the next one on the lap it enters. The tree's mono duty already carries exactly this instrument for the four UTC-window files, so the shape was borrowed rather than invented.
think The ledger was over its 24576 bound at 25589 bytes, which the e123 pin guard was already reporting as pin_over_bound. Folded closed rows 88-95 to crux/archive/ following the ledger's own habit, which brought the pin to 14171 and the guard to verdict=ok in the same act.
think The fold surfaced a second red. reds_ledger_scan.sh counts a row as a table line beginning with a digit cell, and the living ledger stopped writing that shape rounds ago -- its rows are prose now. So the moment the last elder table row folded away, the scan answered rows=0 and verdict=no_rows over a ledger holding ninety-six. The bound failure had been masking the counting failure the whole time, and nothing consumes the scan, so nobody would have seen either.
think Booked it as its own row rather than folding it into %96, since rows are atomic and never edited. Taught the scan the prose shape, and held each kind to its own promise: a full row must name what caught it and what it taught, while a closure note -- which speaks about a row written elsewhere -- must instead name its proof, GREEN or on metal or Released, because a fix closes on a witness rather than a claim. Repointed its default from the work-in-progress symlink to crux/REDS.md.
think Made it stop being a scan nobody runs. The e123 living-pin guard witness already weighed this file's bytes, so it consumes the row scan beside them now -- the ledger and the pin that holds it guarded by one hand. Gave the scan a prove-red mode that generates its control rather than tracking one, so the control can never drift from the shapes the scan enforces.
obs The lesson of %96 is narrower and sharper than measurement-beats-memory. Every duty here was correct; the gap was in what they were pointed at. A guard whose scope is derived from a moving base -- new against origin/main -- shrinks to nothing the moment the work it watches lands, and it keeps reporting GREEN while it watches an empty set. A guard should weigh a standing fact, not a shrinking diff.
obs The two reds are the same shape at two scales, which is why they came up together: a check that cannot see the thing it names will report a confident number forever. Duty 4 could not see a merged stamp; the ledger scan could not see a prose row. Both ran, both passed, both were blind.
obs Rewriting the two future-stamped logs was never on the table and the erratum is not a workaround -- it is the instrument the one-clock law already provides for exactly this, and the ledger row is where the fault is actually recorded.
obs The constructive lap this displaced is designed and ready: the next Caravan rung acts on the verdict rather than only publishing it, re-seating exactly the phases a short plan lost. Reds-first cost it one lap and that is the trade the law is for.
loom witness=one_clock_witness duties=5 head_tolerance_s=120 head_delta_s=1058 red_paths_proven=3
loom witness=equinox_e123_living_pin_guard rows_seen=6 thin_rows=0 reds_bytes=16488 bound=24576 prior_bytes=25589 red_paths_proven=2
loom red=REDS_%96 drift_lap1_s=-120 drift_lap2_s=590 drift_lap3_s=1702
file tools/fixtures/one_clock_head_scan.sh duty 5 -- the living head weighed against the live clock, every run
file tools/fixtures/one_clock_head_erratum.txt the two stamps written ahead of the clock, recorded rather than rewritten
file tools/one_clock_witness.rish duty 5 wired in with both fixtures, GREEN on five duties
file tools/fixtures/reds_ledger_scan.sh the prose row shape, the closure-note proof check, crux/ path, prove-red mode
file tools/gen/season/equinox_e123_living_pin_guard_witness.rish consumes the ledger scan, so it is no longer a scan nobody runs
file crux/REDS.md rows %96 and %97 booked and closed; rows 88-95 folded; tally recounted at 97 rather than carried forward
file crux/archive/REDS-caravan-arc-rows-88-95.md the closed Caravan-arc and Two-Rooms range, every byte kept
file crux/CAIRNS.md the walk-back before the card folded its reckon and harvest edges
file crux/REMEMBER.md live edge refreshed; the reckon and harvest bullets folded into the condensed pointer
recommend keep-going the reds queue is clear again and the next lap is the rung this one displaced -- a supervisor that reads its own short verdict and acts on it, re-seating exactly the arcs the plan lost and never the ones it served, with the mend bounded by the loss so a repair can never quietly re-run work already carried.
